<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Simon bar Kokhba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 135</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Bar Kokhba, Simon bar Kosiba, Bar Kosiba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Simon bar Kokhba, born Simon bar Kosiba, led the Jewish revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a> from 132 to 135 CE. Hailed by some as a messianic figure, 'son of the star', he styled himself Nasi (prince) of Israel and ran an administration known from letters found in Judaean Desert caves. He died when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> took his last stronghold at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/betar">Betar</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/simon-bar-kokhba.jpg" alt="Simon bar Kokhba" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arthur Szyk · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arthur_Szyk_(1894-1951)._Bar_Kochba_(1927),_Paris.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
